  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p-q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 2+{2}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=1,$ and $p-q=-3.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+1=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$",
